// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-10-22 (Thursday): Pure math sequence — the Kolakoski sequence
// ============================================================================
//
// THE SEQUENCE (OEIS A000002): the only sequence over the alphabet {1,2}
// (besides a trivial shift) that is its OWN run-length encoding — read its
// runs of equal symbols and you get the sequence back. Terms 1-16:
//   1 2 2 1 1 2 1 2 2 1 2 2 1 1 2 1
// Check: runs are (1)(2,2)(1,1)(2)(1)(2,2)(1)(2,2)(1,1)(2)(1)... — run
// LENGTHS 1,2,2,1,1,2,1,2,2,1,... are exactly the sequence itself. Terms
// 1-8 sum to 12, terms 9-16 also sum to 12 — a clean two-phrase split.
//
// THE MUSICAL MAPPING plays the sequence's own self-description: the SAME
// 16 terms drive two different musical roles, one role per half of the
// piece, demonstrating "the sequence describes itself" rather than stating
// it in a comment.
//   Cycles 1-2 (terms as RHYTHM): each term is a note's elongation weight
//   (`@1`/`@2`, i.e. a run-length, directly) under a freely-composed call
//   (cycle 1) and response (cycle 2) melody — real leaps (tritone, major
//   3rd), a dip below the tonic, landing unresolved to invite the reply.
//   Cycles 3-4 (terms as PITCH): the SAME 16 terms now pick each note's
//   LEAP SIZE (1 -> up/down a 3rd, 2 -> up/down a tritone) against a
//   hand-placed up/down contour, in even note-values — rhythm traded for
//   pitch, the two roles the sequence's own self-description already
//   proves interchangeable. Cycle 4 dives to the piece's lowest point
//   (degree -7) before climbing back to the tonic.
// Cycles 5-8 are the RETROGRADE (jazz-improv device) of cycles 1-4, note
// order reversed — "reading the sequence backwards" is itself a kind of
// self-reference. The two-note drone (tonic + the step a major 3rd above,
// standing for the sequence's two symbols 1 and 2) enters only here, as
// the piece turns reflective. Retrograde of cycle 4 means the PIECE'S OWN
// ENDING is the low dive followed by silence, not a resolved tonic —
// deliberate: Kolakoski's density/periodicity is still not fully settled
// mathematically, so the piece doesn't resolve neatly either.
//
// SCALE: F:whole:tone — six equal whole-steps, no leading tone, no
// perfect 5th (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md's "E whole tone" Friday precedent, new
// root here). Picked for this topic specifically: a 2-symbol alphabet
// with no internal hierarchy pairs with a scale that has no tonal
// hierarchy either — every step is the same size, same as every symbol
// in {1,2} is just "1" or "2," no scale degree more "central" than another
// except by repetition, same as Kolakoski has no privileged symbol.
//
// ARRANGEMENT: lead is `pulse` (sharp/abrasive, MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §1) —
// unused on a recent Thursday (Padovan: supersaw, Catalan: square) and
// fits a binary/combinatorial topic better than a soft pad. Drone is a
// dyad (tonic + major-3rd-up), not a unison, specifically because the
// alphabet has TWO symbols, not one (contrast Catalan's doubled-UNISON
// drone, which stood for two EQUAL base cases). NO percussion: Kolakoski
// has no known period and no inherent pulse, and the two numbers this
// topic needs an audible outlet for (rhythm, pitch) are already spent as
// the piece's own central device — a drum layer would be a third,
// decorative outlet for numbers already fully heard.
//
// FILTER tracks each cycle's own peak |degree| reached (500 + |degree|*250
// Hz) — a real derived number, not an LFO: cycles 1/2/5/6 peak at degree 6
// (2000 Hz), 3/7 at degree 5 (1750 Hz), 4/8 at the piece's deepest dive,
// degree 7 (2250 Hz). ROOM widens for the retrograde half (1.1 -> 1.6),
// marking "looking back" the same way the piece's own structure splits.
//
// TEMPO: setcpm(24), ~4 beats/cycle = 96 BPM. CYCLES: 8.
// 24 x 8 / 60 = 20s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(24)

$lead: n(`<
  [0@1 3@2 6@2 ~@1 4@1 2@1 0@2 ~@1 -2@1 1@2]
  [1@2 -2@1 0@2 ~@1 2@2 4@1 6@1 ~@1 3@2 0@1]
  [2 5 2 0 2 -1 1 -2 ~]
  [~ -5 -7 -4 -1 1 -1 2 0]
  [1@2 -2@1 ~@1 0@2 2@1 4@1 ~@1 6@2 3@2 0@1]
  [0@1 3@2 ~@1 6@1 4@1 2@2 ~@1 0@2 -2@1 1@2]
  [~ -2 1 -1 2 0 2 5 2]
  [0 2 -1 1 -1 -4 -7 -5 ~]
>`)
  .scale("F:whole:tone")
  .sound("pulse")
  .attack(.01).decay(.12).sustain(.1).release(.18)
  .lpf(`<2000 2000 1750 2250 2000 2000 1750 2250>`)
  .lpq(4)
  .gain(.4)
  .room(`<1.1 1.1 1.1 1.1 1.6 1.6 1.6 1.6>`)

$drone: n("<~ ~ ~ ~ [0,2] [0,2] [0,2] [0,2]>")
  .scale("F:whole:tone")
  .sound("sine")
  .attack(1.2).release(2)
  .lpf(500)
  .gain(.18)
  .room(2.4)
